## Introduction

This article provides a good, conceptual understanding of the history of Unix, BSD, GNU, and Linux from the origins in the 1960s to today.

It focuses on practical, understandable parts. It is not overly long, but still mentions all important details.

## Early History (Before Unix)

### Batch Processing

Early computers in the 1950s did not support multiple users or programs. A person would arrive at the computer,
load program via {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Computer_programming_in_the_punched_card_era", "punched paper cards") }},
load data via magnetic or paper tape, run processing, collect printed output, and leave.

As computers became faster, preparing to run programs and later collecting their results started taking a large percentage of time.
So programs called *monitors*, the precursors to operating systems, were developed to process series or "batches" of programs.
Batches were prepared on {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Magnetic-tape_data_storage", "magnetic tapes") }} in advance, saving available computer time for actual computation.

This method later became known as {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Batch_processing", "batch processing") }}.

In practice, batch processing was inconvenient. It still required inputting both program code and data to process, it didn't support interactive use, and users typically didn't even have direct access to machines &mdash; they gave programs to *operators* who scheduled them on available computers and later handed back the results or failure reports. If direct access was available, it was only during the night in single user mode, while the operators were off duty and the machines were kept running 24/7 to avoid power-cycling tens or even hundreds of kilowatts-rated systems.

### Software Licensing

In the 1950s and 1960s, software and compilers were delivered as part of hardware purchases without separate fees.

By purchasing hardware, customers became tied to the vendor-provided ecosystem. Contracts included everything needed for a running installation -- support, engineering time, debugging tools, etc.. And source code made fixing bugs or adding missing features possible.

<!-- The code was available but generally not {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Free_and_open-source_software", "free and open source software") }}. -->
The first example of {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Free_and_open-source_software", "free and open source software") }} is believed to have been the {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/A-0_System", "A-2 system") }}, developed at the {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/UNIVAC", "UNIVAC") }} division of {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Remington_Rand", "Remington Rand") }} in 1953. Customers were invited to send source code improvements back to UNIVAC.

Later, almost all IBM mainframe software was also distributed with source code.

Some university computer labs had a policy of requiring all installed programs to come with published source code.

Computer manufacturers were also supportive of their customers forming user groups. {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/IBM_701", "IBM 701") }} and {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/IBM_704", "IBM 704") }} customers had {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/SHARE_user_group", "SHARE") }} user group founded in 1955 and {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Digital_Equipment_Corporation", "Digital Equipment Corporation (DEC)") }} had {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/DECUS", "DECUS") }} user group founded in 1961. Into the 1970s there were a number of user groups formed to get users involved and facilitate the exchange of software.

## Leading up to Unix

With somewhat better availability and lower prices of computers, users wanted to replace batch processing with direct, interactive access.

This was done using two different approaches. One approach was to build single-user {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Microcomputer", "microcomputers") }} as cheaply as possible, disregarding their low memory and performance, and sell them to hobbyists.

The other approach was to enable multiple users to share bigger, more capable machines. Those were {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mainframe", "mainframes") }} and {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Minicomputers", "minicomputers") }}. In the early 1960s the first advanced operating systems became available and they soon had
{{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Time-sharing", "time-sharing") }}, {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Computer_multitasking", "multi-tasking") }}, and {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Multi-user_software", "multi-user") }} characteristics.

First such systems were:

### Compatible Time-Sharing System (CTSS)

In 1961 at {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Massachusetts_Institute_of_Technology", "MIT") }}, {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_McCarthy_(computer_scientist)", "John McCarthy") }}, {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jack_Dennis", "Jack Dennis") }}, and {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fernando_J._Corbat%C3%B3", "Fernando Corbató") }} talked about allowing multiple computer users to use a system interactively and behave as if they were the only user.

In November 1961, Corbató's {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Compatible_Time-Sharing_System", "Compatible Time-Sharing System (CTSS)") }} was the first modern, multi-user, general-purpose system, built on a modified {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/IBM_709", "IBM 709") }} computer.
("Compatible Time Sharing" was referring to compatibility with batch processing -- the OS could support both at the same time.)

There exists a recorded {{ L("https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Q07PhW5sCEk", "video demonstration and explanation of CTSS at MIT from 1963") }}.

Installations of early operating systems could be counted by hand. For example, for more than 10 years CTSS ran on only two machines at MIT, with some outside users
connecting from California, South America, the University of Edinburgh, and the University of Oxford.

CTSS' novel features are summarized in the Wikipedia article {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Compatible_Time-Sharing_System#Features", "CTSS Features") }}.

### Berkeley Timesharing System (BTS)

{{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Berkeley_Timesharing_System", "Berkeley Timesharing System") }} was a time-sharing operating system implemented between 1964 and 1967 as part of {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Project_Genie", "Project Genie") }} at the {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/University_of_California%2C_Berkeley", "University of California, Berkeley (UCB)") }}. It was the first commercial timesharing system that allowed general-purpose user programming.

Students who worked on the Berkeley Timesharing System included {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chuck_Thacker", "Charles P. Thacker") }}, {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/L._Peter_Deutsch", "L. Peter Deutsch") }}, and {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Butler_Lampson", "Butler Lampson") }}.

Speaking of BTS' features that influenced Unix, BTS consisted of a _monitor_ (roughly an OS {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kernel_(operating_system)", "kernel") }}) and an _executive_ (roughly a command-line interface), _fork()_ and _exec()_ calls were separate, and the text editor {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/QED_(text_editor)", "QED") }} (a predecessor of {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ed_(text_editor)", "ed") }}, {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ex_(text_editor)", "ex") }}, and {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vi_(text_editor)", "vi") }}) was first implemented for BTS.

### Multiplexed Information and Computing Service (Multics)

{{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Multics", "Multics") }} was another early time-sharing
operating system started in 1964 to fix and improve on CTSS' design. It was a cooperative project between {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Project_MAC", "MIT CSAIL's Project MAC") }}, {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/General_electric", "General Electric") }},
and {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bell_Labs", "Bell Labs") }}. (Bell Labs at the time was a subsidiary of {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/AT%26T", "AT&T") }} and AT&T's manufacturing and supply company {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Western_Electric", "Western Electric") }}.)

However, Multics required special hardware (a {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/GE_645", "GE 645") }} machine), and the overall design proved to be expensive and impractical.

Bell withdrew from the project after 5 years, in 1969, as it became clear that Multics would not reach their goals in the short term.
Shortly after, in 1970, General Electric sold its computer division to {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Honeywell", "Honeywell") }} who offered Multics commercially with limited success.
The project continued as it was also funded by the US government who demanded a new, secure operating system. The last Multics installation was shut down in the year 2000.

{{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ken_Thompson", "Ken Thompson") }} later described Multics as a project that tried to carry out research in not one but four different areas at the same time, and that was "overdesigned and overbuilt and over everything, close to unusable".

Multics' novel features are summarized in the Wikipedia article {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Multics#Novel_ideas", "Multics Novel Ideas") }}.

### Incompatible Timesharing System (ITS)

{{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Incompatible_Timesharing_System", "Incompatible Timesharing System (ITS)") }} was a time-sharing operating system started in 1967 and developed principally by the {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/MIT_AI_Lab", "MIT AI Lab") }} and {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Project_MAC", "MIT CSAIL Project MAC") }}.

Its development was initiated by staff who disagreed with the direction taken by Project MAC's Multics. The word "Incompatible" was chosen as a humorous complement to the MIT's earlier "Compatible" Time-Sharing System (CTSS).

ITS was written in the assembly language for {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/PDP-6", "PDP-6") }} and later {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/PDP-10", "PDP-10") }} {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mainframe", "mainframes") }}.
(DEC PDP-10s were popular in education, research, and on the {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/ARPANET", "ARPANet") }}, and were hugely influential on later hardware and software.)

The wide-open philosophy of ITS and its collaborative online community were a further major influence on the {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hacker_culture", "hacker culture") }}, as described in {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hackers:_Heroes_of_the_Computer_Revolution", "Steven Levy's book 'Hackers'") }}. They were predecessors of the {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Free_and_open-source_software", "free and open-source software") }}, {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Open-design_movement", "open design") }}, and {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wiki", "Wiki") }} movements.

ITS' novel features are summarized in the Wikipedia article {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Incompatible_Timesharing_System#Significant_technical_features", "ITS Significant Technical Features") }}.

### TENEX

{{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/TENEX_(operating_system)", "TENEX") }} was an operating system started in 1969 by {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Raytheon_BBN", "Bolt Beranek Newman (BBN)") }} for DEC PDP-10 machines. The name, short for "ten-extended", came from its initial relation to (and an upgrade path from) the DEC {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/TOPS-10", "TOPS-10") }} operating system. By 1976, TENEX moved to DEC and was released in their new and different operating system {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tops-20", "TOPS-20") }}.

TENEX was based heavily on ideas from {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Multics", "Multics") }} and the mentioned {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Project_Genie", "Project Genie") }}. From Genie it borrowed almost identical code and then improved {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Memory_management", "memory management") }} with {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mmap", "mmapping") }}, {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Demand_paging", "demand paging") }}, and automatic {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Page_fault", "page fault") }} restarts.
It also brought general improvements to the {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hierarchical_file_system", "hierarchical file system") }}, {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Access-control_list", "access control lists") }}, remote file access, program debugging and tracing, terminal handling and line editing, and implemented user conveniences such as command line {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Command-line_completion", "completion") }}, {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Command_history", "history") }}, and abbreviations (aliasing).

CTSS, BTS, Multics, ITS, and TENEX have influenced all later operating systems, from {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mainframe", "mainframes") }} to {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Microcomputer", "microcomputers") }}.

## Unix

### What is Unix?

Initially, {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Unix", "Unix") }} was a general-purpose {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Time-sharing", "time-sharing") }}, {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Computer_multitasking", "multi-tasking") }}, {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Multi-user_software", "multi-user") }} operating system started in 1968 at the {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bell_Labs", "AT&T Bell Labs") }} research center. It included the Unix {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kernel_(operating_system)", "kernel") }} and a set of utilities for the whole system to be useful. In the period from 1971 to 1989 Bell Labs published 10 releases of Unix, later named {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Research_Unix", "Research Unix") }}.

But AT&T at the time was a telecommunications monopoly in the US. Its phone service fees were regulated, and by a {{ L("https://www.nytimes.com/1956/01/25/archives/att-settles-antitrust-case-shares-patents-us-hails-consent-decree.html", "Jan 1956") }} {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Consent_decree", "consent decree") }} AT&T was prohibited from expanding commercially into other industries, and was obliged to license its 8,600 existing and future patents to others without collecting royalties. So AT&T licensed Unix source code to numerous companies and universities, who in turn created their own, conceptually similar, but separate operating systems having names such as {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Berkeley_Software_Distribution", "BSD") }}, {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sunos", "SunOS") }}, {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Solaris_(operating_system)", "Solaris") }}, {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ultrix", "Ultrix") }}, {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Irix", "IRIX") }}, {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/HP-UX", "HP-UX") }}, {{ L("https://gunkies.org/wiki/Dell_UNIX", "Dell UNIX") }}, {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/AIX", "AIX") }}, {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/DYNIX", "DYNIX") }}, {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tru64_UNIX", "Digital UNIX") }}, {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/NeXTSTEP", "NeXTSTEP") }}, and {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/MacOS", "macOS") }}.

When Unix appeared in the '70s, it revolutionized the industry:

- Quoting {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ken_Thompson", "Ken Thompson") }}, "It meant the beginning of an end of large computer centers where bureaucracy behind multi-million-dollar machines controlled computing". Unlike for the expensive {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mainframe", "mainframes") }}, Unix was intended for, comparatively, more affordable and practical {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Minicomputers", "minicomputers") }} and, later, {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Workstation", "workstations") }}

- It was multi-platform. Before Unix, computer manufacturers typically provided their own operating system bundled with the hardware and there was no any compatibility between manufacturers; sometimes not even between offerings from the same manufacturer. Now users could obtain any computer and install or port Unix to it

- It was small and understandable. It could be read line by line, modified, recompiled, and reinstalled, giving users total control over the whole system

- And its source code was available to universities for a nominal fee, generating long-lasting interest, enthusiasm, and hacker culture.
This later translated into long-lasting business demand and, as an offshoot, also created the Unix workstation market that dominated the IT industry in the period from 1985 to 1995

But those were circumstantial benefits. The true significance of Unix was in its design, which was based on simple and incredibly powerful abstractions.
It provided users with a unique combination of convenience, simplicity, and power unmatched by any other system, which later became known as the {{ R("unix-philosophy", "Unix philosophy") }}.

As Dennis Ritchie wrote in {{ L("https://dl.acm.org/doi/10.1145/361011.361061", "The UNIX time-sharing system") }} in 1974, "Unix contained a number of features very seldom offered even by larger systems".
Or as {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Doug_McIlroy", "Doug McIlroy") }} said, "Unix was an improvement on its predecessors and most of its successors".

After ~20 years of success, in the late 1990s Unix saw a sharp and permanent commercial decline, but it already inspired creation of numerous {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Unix-like", "Unix-like") }} operating systems that followed the {{ R("unix-philosophy", "Unix philosophy") }}, but did not contain any AT&T code. Those systems have since replaced and far surpassed the original Unix.

Most important Unix-like operating systems today are the open source {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gnu/linux", "GNU/Linux") }} distributions, {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bsd", "BSD") }} distributions released after 1994, and {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Android_(operating_system)", "Android") }}.
Notable proprietary OSes are {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/MacOS", "Apple macOS") }} (and its embedded/mobile variants), traditional Unixes {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oracle_Solaris", "Oracle Solaris") }}, {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/HP-UX", "HP-UX") }}, and {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/IBM_AIX", "IBM AIX") }}, and a real-time Unix-like OS {{ L("https://blackberry.qnx.com/", "QNX") }}.

Today, the term Unix can be used informally to refer to all Unix and Unix-like systems. Their exact names and full listing can be seen in the {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_Unix_systems", "List of Unix systems") }}.

### Origins of Unix

{{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/AT%26T_Corporation", "AT&T Corporation") }}, once the world's largest telecommunications company and employing a million people, was established in 1885 through merging of local telephone companies across the United States. To meet the challenges of providing nation-wide telecommunication services, in 1925 it established {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bell_Labs", "Bell Labs") }} as a research and development subsidiary.

The genius of {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bell_Labs", "Bell Labs") }}' management and staff cannot be overstated.
Throughout the 20th century, Bell Labs and its researchers have been credited with the development of radio astronomy, transistor, laser, photovoltaic cell, cellphone, communications satellites, charge-coupled device (CCD), information theory, Unix, programming languages B, C, C++, S, SNOBOL, AWK, AMPL, and others. Eleven {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nobel_Prize", "Nobel Prizes") }} and five {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Turing_Award", "Turing Awards") }} have been awarded for work completed at Bell Labs.

In 1969, after a withdrawal from the expensive and failed project {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Multics", "Multics") }}, the last remaining Bell Labs' Multics participants
{{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ken_Thompson", "Ken Thompson") }},
{{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dennis_Ritchie", "Dennis Ritchie") }},
{{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Joe_Ossanna", "Joe Ossanna") }}, and the department head
{{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Doug_McIlroy", "Doug McIlroy") }}
found themselves without a primary project to work on, but remained interested in operating systems.
Thompson and Ritchie wanted a simple, powerful, interactive system with a good programming environment, around which a user community could form.

At Bell Labs, 26-year old Thompson found a group of about 10 geographically separate, rarely used, and by then obsolete {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/PDP-7", "DEC PDP-7") }} minicomputers. They were used as remote job entry stations for electric circuits analysis -- data would be input into PDP-7s and sent via telephone lines at 2000 baud to the main {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/IBM_7090#IBM_7094", "IBM 7094") }} machine for computation. Thompson used the PDP-7 machine at hand, whose configuration costed ~$250k in 1965, to get familiar with the PDP-7 architecture by porting games:

Ritchie's historical record {{ L("https://www.bell-labs.com/usr/dmr/www/hist.html", "The Evolution of the Unix Time-sharing System") }} mentions a port of 2D game {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Space_Travel_(video_game)", "Space Travel") }}, which Thompson had previously written for a {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/GE-600_series", "GE 635") }} machine and which Ritchie described in more detail in {{ L("https://www.nokia.com/bell-labs/about/dennis-m-ritchie/spacetravel.html", "Space Travel: Exploring the solar system and the PDP-7") }}. Thompson and Ritchie used the GE machine to create a paper-tape executable of Space Travel that would run directly on the PDP-7 hardware.

{{ L("https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=EY6q5dv_B-o", "Thompson's 2019 interview") }} mentions another, later game, a 3D clone of {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Spacewar!", "Spacewar!") }}, which was originally a 2D game written by {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Steve_Russell_(computer_scientist)", "Steve Russell") }} for a {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/PDP-1", "DEC PDP-1") }} minicomputer. Like the original Spacewar!, Thompson's game was multi-player, played via telephone lines between any two machines in the group. The space was a 3D cube and large; players sometimes exchanged coordinates so they could fly towards and find each other.

The PDP-7 machine also happened to have a high-performance disk. Thompson wrote a scheduling algorithm to maximize throughput from it, then wrote the surrounding test and support utilities, and in cooperation with {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rudd_Canaday", "Rudd Canaday") }} developed a filesystem. Finally, he realized he was three weeks away from a self-supported operating system. While his wife and child were away for three weeks visiting his family and relatives on the US west coast, Thompson added the _exec_ call to differentiate between programs and data, and then wrote a loader, an editor, and an assembler, each in one week. (Some sources report "shell" or "kernel overlay" instead of "loader".) With an in-kernel {{ L("https://gunkies.org/wiki/Shell", "shell") }} for two stations (the localhost and a {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Teletype_Model_33", "Model 33 teletype") }}), the prototype {{ L("https://gunkies.org/wiki/PDP-7_UNIX", "Version 0 Unix") }} came into existence.

({{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Teletype_Model_33", "Model 33 teletype") }} was a computer-controlled electro-mechanical typewriter that passed any key presses to the computer and printed any output to paper rolls, in uppercase, 10 characters per second. A later {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Teletype_Model_37", "Model 37") }} introduced a complete character set, lowercase letters, and a 50% speed improvement, but as  {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brian_Kernighan", "Brian Kernighan") }} said, both models were "an upper-body exercise to use". Their manufacturer, {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Teletype_Corporation", "Teletype Corporation") }}, was part of AT&T and teletypes were used widely for communication and connecting to computers.)

> The name Unix (originally Unics) is itself a pun on Multics.
> The U in Unix is rumored to stand for *uniplexed* as opposed to the *multiplexed* of Multics,
> further underscoring the designers' rejections of Multics' complexity in favor of a more
> straightforward and workable approach for smaller computers.

{{ L("https://gunkies.org/wiki/PDP-7_UNIX", "V0 Unix") }} existed for about one year and ran on {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/PDP-7", "DEC PDP-7") }}, two {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/PDP-9", "DEC PDP-9") }}, and one {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/PDP-15", "DEC PDP-15") }} computer at Bell Labs. There is a {{ L("archive/www.youtube.com/LCM+L PDP-7 booting and running UNIX Version 0 [pvaPaWyiuLA].webm", "video of V0 Unix running on a PDP-7") }} available.

Based on experience from Multics, Bell Labs was no longer interested in funding operating systems research, so Thompson's group's request for a purchase of {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/PDP-10", "DEC PDP-10") }} or {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/SDS_Sigma_series", "SDS Sigma 7") }} mainframe was not approved and the work on Unix initially went on without management support.

The group continued developing Unix on the PDP-7, and even though it was ~10 times slower than other machines in the computer center, the system was used by impressive names -- Thompson, Ritchie, McIlroy, {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Robert_Morris_(cryptographer)", "Robert Morris") }} senior, and few others, up to two users at a time.

In the early 1970, Thompson and Ritchie managed to get funding for a just-announced {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/PDP-11", "DEC PDP-11/20") }} with 24 kB of primary memory (16 kB for the OS, 8 kB for programs) and 512 kB disk. 
The configuration ordered by the department costed about $65,000 at the time, which was a magnitude less than other proposals the group had submitted without success in the previous one or two years (although all were well within budget).

But the key to getting the request approved was not the amount. (AT&T was spending an estimated 2.8% on research and development, and Bell Labs was financed with 1% from tax on telephones and calls in the US. Bell Labs employees were entitled to personal yearly budget in the amount equal to their yearly fully loaded salary, and it was easy to combine 4 or 5 people to reach multi-million dollar budgets for projects.)

Rather, the key was {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Joe_Ossanna", "Joe Ossanna") }}'s idea to involve the patents department.
The department needed an adequate system for producing and managing patent applications. As no software was capable of fulfilling the requirements and also auto-formatting text, printing math symbols, and printing line numbers on pages,
they were planning to purchase a specialized computer.

The Unix group proposed to do it in-house and, as Thompson says, "it was to save money, it wasn't about operating systems (honest! :)), and it was for somebody else. It was a 3-way win impossible to say no to".
On a side note, these text processing needs were why {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Joe_Ossanna", "Joe Ossanna") }} wrote {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Roff_(software)", "nroff and troff") }} document processing systems very early on, and why Unix always had powerful text processing functions.

The project was a great success, supporting concurrent work of 6 users (3 patent editors and the Unix group). A second PDP-11/20 was soon ordered to keep the research and patents work separate, and Unix also started spreading to various AT&T departments.

Unix was originally written in {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Assembler_(computing)", "assembly language") }}.
As assembler is architecture-specific, Unix couldn't be ported to other computer architectures without essentially rewriting it.

In 1972, {{ L("https://gunkies.org/wiki/PDP-11/45", "DEC PDP-11/45") }} computer was released and Unix rewritten for it. It was the first hardware able to support Unix without obvious deficiencies and lacking features. Dennis Ritchie designed the {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/C_(programming_language)", "C programming language") }} to separate Unix from platform-specific implementation details.

In 1973, {{ L("https://gunkies.org/wiki/PDP-11/40", "DEC PDP-11/40") }} computer was released and Unix ported to it. The Unix kernel was rewritten in C after two or three failed attempts, enabled by the addition of {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Struct_(C_programming_language)", "structs") }} to the C language. Utilities were rewritten over the next couple years.

In 1975, {{ L("https://gunkies.org/wiki/PDP-11/70", "DEC PDP-11/70") }} computer was released and Unix ported to it.

In 1977, Unix was ported to {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/IBM_System/360_Model_91", "IBM System 360 Model 91") }} and 32-bit {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Interdata_8/32", "Interdata 7/32 and 8/32") }}, the first platforms other than the DEC PDP line.

In 1978, {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brian_Kernighan", "Brian Kernighan") }} and {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dennis_Ritchie", "Dennis Ritchie") }} co-authored the book {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_C_Programming_Language", "The C Programming Language") }}, 1st edition.

Starting with the early 1980s and enabled by advancements in hardware, a minimum requirement for Unix workstations became the "3M" -- a megabyte of primary memory, a megapixel display, and a speed of one 1 {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/FLOPS", "MFLOPS") }}.

### AT&T / Bell Labs Unix Releases

As mentioned, in the period from 1971 to 1989 Bell Labs published 10 releases of Unix, later named {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Research_Unix", "Research Unix") }}.

Internally at Bell Labs, Unix did not have releases or versions -- it was a continuously developed system.

When {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Doug_McIlroy", "Doug McIlroy") }} organized "{{ L("https://web.archive.org/web/20200601064923/https://www.bell-labs.com/usr/dmr/www/manintro.html", "Unix Programmer's Manuals (UPM)") }}" to be written for the PDP-11 version, editions of manual became versions of Unix they were describing.

The editions of Unix Programmer's Manuals were:

- Nov 1971 - {{ L("https://gunkies.org/wiki/UNIX_First_Edition", "Unix First Edition") }}. The OS contained ~30 system calls in the kernel, half of which were filesystem-related

- Jun 1972 - {{ L("https://gunkies.org/wiki/UNIX_Second_Edition", "Unix Second edition") }}. {{ L("https://www.tuhs.org/Archive/Distributions/Research/Dennis_v2/v2man.pdf", "Unix Programmer's Manual, 2nd edition") }} mentions 10 installations of Unix within AT&T

- Feb 1973 - {{ L("https://gunkies.org/wiki/UNIX_Third_Edition", "Unix Third Edition") }}. The manual mentions 16 installations of Unix within AT&T. Unix {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Unix_pipe", "pipes") }} were released with this version

- Nov 1973 - {{ L("https://gunkies.org/wiki/UNIX_Fourth_Edition", "Unix Fourth Edition") }}. The first version with Unix kernel written in the C programming language. Ritchie and Thompson presented the first paper on Unix at the {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Symposium_on_Operating_Systems_Principles", "Symposium on Operating System Principles (SOSP)") }}

- Jun 1974 - {{ L("https://gunkies.org/wiki/UNIX_Fifth_Edition", "Unix Fifth Edition") }}. The manual mentions more than 50 installations. That year Thompson and Ritchie submitted paper {{ L("https://dl.acm.org/doi/10.1145/361011.361061", "The UNIX time-sharing system") }} to the {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Communications_of_the_ACM", "Communications of the ACM (CACM)") }} journal. It caused requests for Unix to start coming in in piles. It was the first version to be distributed externally to a limited extent, and academic institutions could get source code with a license permitting educational use

- May 1975 - {{ L("https://gunkies.org/wiki/UNIX_Sixth_Edition", "Unix Sixth Edition") }}. V6 was implemented in only 9073 lines of code, although without a full-screen text editor and a windowing system. It seemed like a mainframe OS on a minicomputer, and was highly influential: it became the first version of Unix ported to non-PDP, 32-bit computers, about 90% of all code was in C, John Lions used it for his {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lions%27_Commentary_on_UNIX_6th_Edition,_with_Source_Code", "commentary on the UNIX Operating System") }}, it was the basis of multiple forks including {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Berkeley_Software_Distribution", "BSD") }}, it was used by ~100 universities, and it also had a commercial version called {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/PWB/UNIX", "PWB/UNIX") }}

- Jan 1979 - {{ L("https://gunkies.org/wiki/Unix_Seventh_Edition", "Unix Seventh Edition") }}. This was the first "big" release of Unix after the system became multi-platform in 1977, grew much larger than V6, and attracted more interest from AT&T lawyers. {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stephen_C._Johnson", "Steve Johnson (Yaccman)") }} called it "the first portable Unix"; over time it was ported to countless varieties of hardware. Most 3rd party licensees from this period derived their Unix from V6 or V7. At the same time, it was the last highly-influential {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Research_Unix", "Research Unix") }} release

- Feb 1985 - {{ L("https://gunkies.org/wiki/Unix_Eighth_Edition", "Unix Eighth Edition") }}. AT&T based this version on {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Berkeley_Software_Distribution", "BSD") }} to obtain a couple big features that its Unix kernels were missing, and to try regain control of the Unix market. Unix was originally spelled "UNIX", which changed to "Unix" by the time of {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Version_8_Unix", "Version 8 Unix") }}

- Sep 1986 - {{ L("https://gunkies.org/wiki/Unix_Ninth_Edition", "Unix Ninth Edition") }}

- Oct 1989 - {{ L("https://gunkies.org/wiki/Unix_Tenth_Edition", "Unix Tenth Edition") }}. It was not distributed to external parties

### Early Licensing and Distribution, from ~1970 to ~1980

The history and evolution of Unix cannot be understood without an insight into its licensing.

In 1949, the {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_States_Department_of_Justice", "US Department of Justice") }} filed suit against AT&T and its subsidiary {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Western_Electric", "Western Electric") }}, claiming that companies were {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Monopoly", "monopolies") }} acting "in restraint of trade".

In 1956, by the mentioned antitrust {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Consent_decree", "consent decree") }}, the companies were prohibited from branching into areas other than those used in providing telephone, telegraph, and common carrier communications services, and AT&T was also required to license 8,600 patents it held to interested parties without collecting royalties.

As {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Peter_H._Salus", "Peter H. Salus") }} wrote in his book {{ L("https://web.archive.org/web/20240701004249/http://www.groklaw.net/staticpages/index.php?page=20051013231901859", "The Daemon, the GNU & the Penguin") }}, "no one could have foreseen the problems that this consent decree would entail", referring to the far-reaching impact this decision had on Unix licensing 20 years later.

In October 1973, Dennis Ritchie and Ken Thompson visited the new {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thomas_J._Watson_Research_Center", "IBM Research Center at Yorktown Heights in Hudson Valley") }} to deliver the mentioned first paper on Unix at the {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Symposium_on_Operating_Systems_Principles", "Symposium on Operating System Principles (SOSP)") }}.
The audience asked for copies of the new system, which raised a question -- was a computer operating system part of "common carrier communications services", and was AT&T required to distribute Unix?

Since Unix wasn't related to communications services, the decision of corporate lawyers was that Bell Labs should distribute Unix to academic and research institutions for free, at the cost of media plus shipping, which was altogether set at {{ L("https://www.in2013dollars.com/us/inflation/1973?amount=150", "$150 in 1973's money") }}. The policy was “no advertising, no support, no bug fixes, and payment in advance”.

Licensing was organized through Western Electric, and within a few months several dozen institutions requested Unix. (But note that, although the distribution included source code, it was not nearly {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Free_software", "Free Software") }}. Unix was AT&T's {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trade_secret", "trade secret") }} and the license, among other things, did not grant the permission to redistribute Unix or its modified versions.)

In 1974, unrelated to Unix, US DOJ filed another antitrust case against AT&T in {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_States_v._AT%26T", "United States v. AT&T") }}.

In early 1974, Mel Ferentz and Lou Katz organized a meeting of Unix users in New York. The agenda was simple: reports of Unix installations and purposes; lunch; "Ken Thompson speaks!"; Unix hints; DEC hints; open discussion. There were 20 to 40 participants. Meetings kept being held twice yearly in a very informal fashion, facilitating sharing of software, bug reports, and bug fixes.

In 1975, those meetings turned into a non-profit organization "UNIX Users Group". In 1977, the group changed name to {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/USENIX", "USENIX: The Advanced Computing Systems Association") }}, after a letter from a lawyer stating that AT&T had not granted the permission to use "UNIX" in "UNIX Users Group". In the same year and for the same reason, Ferentz' {{ L("https://www.usenix.org/system/files/login/articles/login_apr15_16_unix_news.pdf", "UNIX News") }} newsletter was renamed to {{ L("https://www.usenix.org/publications/loginonline", ";login:") }}. (The name came from a quirk and prompt displayed when Unix booted and was ready to accept user logins.)

Unix was becoming popular and AT&T wanted to profit from it. But first they were not allowed to do so due to antitrust lawsuits, and later after 1982-1984 they didn't know how to do it successfully. As a result, the history of traditional Unix became inseparable from the effects that AT&T's increasingly strict, expensive, and conflicting licensing had on it from roughly 1977-1978 onwards.

### Berkeley Software Distribution (BSD) Unix

In 1973, {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bob_Fabry", "Bob Fabry") }} from the {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/University_of_California%2C_Berkeley", "University of California, Berkeley (UCB)") }} attended the mentioned Ritchie and Thompson's SOSP talk.

In 1974, AT&T's Unix source code and license arrived to UCB.

In 1975, Ken Thompson took a sabbatical from Bell Labs and came to Berkeley as a visiting professor. He helped install Version 6 Unix.
In parallel, students doing operating systems research modified and extended Unix.

Other universities became interested in software from Berkeley, and so in 1977-1978 {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bill_Joy", "Bill Joy") }}, a graduate student, started compiling the first
{{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Berkeley_Software_Distribution", "Berkeley Software Distribution (BSD)") }}.

In 1978, 1BSD was released as an addon for Version 6 Unix. About thirty copies were sent out.

Later in 1978, 2BSD was released. Over 70 copies were sent out.

That was being done with AT&T's approval, and because BSD contained copyrighted AT&T Unix source code, it was only available to organizations who already had the Unix source code license from AT&T.

Thompson, Ritchie, and {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stephen_C._Johnson", "Steve Johnson") }} were unhappy with the treatment they were receiving from {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Digital_Equipment_Corporation", "DEC") }}, and have refused DEC's offer to port Unix to {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/VAX", "DEC VAX") }} 32-bit machines. So DEC turned to another group inside Bell Labs, at Holmdel, where John F. Reiser and Tom London based their port on V7 Unix and called it {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/UNIX/32V", "Unix/32V") }}. Unix/32V did not have support for {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Virtual_memory", "virtual memory (swap)") }} in VAX, but was widely used and got requested by about 6 universities. AT&T approved it to be sent to one -- the UCB.

At the end of 1978, before the official release, Unix/32V arrived to UCB.

In 1979, 3BSD was released, and since it was based on Unix/32V and many components were rewritten and improved for 32-bit, including adding the {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Virtual_memory", "virtual memory (swap)") }} support, it became a complete system rather than an addon. The popularity of 3BSD also made other Berkeley extensions well-known, such as the {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/C_shell", "C shell") }}, {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Curses_(programming_library)", "curses") }}, {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Termcap", "termcap") }}, {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vi_(text_editor)", "vi") }}, and {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Job_control_%28Unix%29", "job control") }}.

In 1979-1980, {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Darpa", "DARPA") }} was interested in an enhanced and supported version of the already-portable 3BSD for their use.
Fabry made a proposal to DARPA and in some consultation with {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Raytheon_BBN", "Bolt Beranek Newman (BBN)") }} (the developers of {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/ARPANET", "ARPANet") }}) won an 18-month contract.

As part of the contract, the {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Computer_Systems_Research_Group", "Computer Systems Research Group (CSRG)") }} at UCB was formed, and it continued working on BSD.

A more complete history of BSD Unix can be read in the {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/History_of_the_Berkeley_Software_Distribution", "History of the Berkeley Software Distribution") }} or in the article {{ L("https://www.abortretry.fail/p/the-berkley-software-distribution", "The Berkeley Software Distribution") }}, but in summary, the following BSD releases could be mentioned as significant:

For PDP-11:

- 2.8.1BSD - the version of V7 Unix (initially slower than V6) that was improved by users and announced	at USENIX in 1982

- 2.11BSD - the last BSD release for PDP-11, from 1991, still maintained with patches available on {{ L("https://www.tuhs.org/Archive/Distributions/UCB/2.11BSD/Patches/?C=M;O=D", "TUHS.ORG - 2.11BSD - Patches") }}

For 32-bit DEC VAX and later computers:

- 3BSD - derived from Unix/32V, with support for virtual memory (swap), from 1979

- 4BSD - vastly improved 3BSD, from 1980, and de-facto standard Unix for VAX before AT&T's release of {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Unix_System_III", "System III") }}. (Users would get a Unix/32V license from AT&T and then obtain 4BSD)

- 4.1cBSD - version tuned for performance by {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bill_Joy", "Bill Joy") }}, from 1983. It ran some benchmarks as fast as the then-predominant OS for DEC VAX, the {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/OpenVMS", "DEC VMS") }}. 4.1cBSD became the basis for {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ultrix", "DEC Ultrix") }}, {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/SunOS", "SunOS") }}, and AT&T's {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Version_8_Unix", "Version 8 Unix") }}

- 4.2BSD - the first BSD released under the leadership of {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mike_Karels", "Mike Karels") }} and {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Marshall_Kirk_McKusick", "Marshall Kirk McKusick") }}, from 1983, after {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bill_Joy", "Bill Joy") }} left in 1982 to co-found {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sun_Microsystems", "Sun Microsystems") }}. This should have been named 5BSD, but Berkeley kept it at 4.X to avoid discussing licensing with AT&T for a new major version. Users ended up having to be relicensed, though, due to code from AT&T's new release that was included

- 4.4BSD-Lite - the first BSD release free of AT&T's intellectual property, from 1994

- {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/SunOS", "SunOS") }} 4.1.4 - the last release of {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sun_Microsystems", "Sun Microsystems") }}' OS based on BSD, from 1994. (Later versions were based on {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/System_V_Release_4#SVR4", "AT&T System V Release 4 (SVR4)") }} and renamed {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Solaris_(operating_system)", "Solaris") }})

- {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Berkeley_Software_Distribution#Standards", "4.4BSD-Lite2") }} - the second BSD release free of AT&T's intellectual property, from June 1995, and also the last one from UCB, because CSRG was disbanded shortly after

### Unix and ARPANet (the Internet)

By 1975, {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/ARPANET", "ARPANet") }} contained 60 nodes and was soon to become {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Internet", "the Internet") }}. In May 1975, Steve Holmgren's {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Request_for_Comments", "RFC") }} 681 "{{ L("https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc681.txt", "Network UNIX") }}" appeared, stating:

> The Unix time-sharing system presents several interesting capabilities as an ARPA network mini-host. It offers powerful local processing facilities in terms of user programs, several compilers, an editor based on Qed, a versatile document preparation system, and an efficient file system featuring sophisticated access control, mountable and de-mountable volumes, and a unified treatment of peripherals as special files.

The result was that Internet started running on Unix. The protocols were also in tune with the {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Unix_Philosophy", "Unix philosophy") }}. Anyone running Unix had accessible source code, and there were true communication and interoperability. Unix was used throughout the world -- Japan and Australia, most of Europe, and North America.

The number of systems connected to ARPANet over the years grew as follows:

- 1976: 63

- 1981: >200

- 1982: 235

- 1983: 562

- 1984: 1024

- 1986: 2308

- 1987: 28174

- 1992: 727000

### John Lions at UNSW, Australia

In 1974-1975, professor {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_Lions", "John Lions") }} from the Australian {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/University_of_New_South_Wales", "University of New South Wales (UNSW)") }} became interested in Unix after reading Thompson and Ritchie's CACM paper, and his institution obtained Unix source code.

Lions wanted to use Unix for teaching operating systems, but there were no textbooks. The existing Unix documentation did not discuss its internals or operating system design.

In 1976, he wrote a {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lions%27_Commentary_on_UNIX_6th_Edition,_with_Source_Code", "A Commentary on the UNIX Operating System") }} for {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Version_6_Unix", "Version 6 Unix") }} with source code included (9073 lines of code at the time). He received permission from AT&T to print out the code and commentary for instructional purposes.

In March 1977, UNIX News announced the availability of the book to licensees, together with a note by Mel Ferentz: "Ken Thompson has seen the first version of the book and reports that it is a good job".

The book became as popular as Unix itself. Lions shipped around 600 copies, including an order of 200 to Bell Labs.

AT&T’s attention was drawn to the book. With Lions' agreement, in 1978 an announcement was made that further orders should be placed with Bell Laboratories. AT&T soon changed their mind and within the same year announced that books were no longer available.

As a result, over the next two decades John Lions' book was shared privately and was half-jokingly said to have become the most copied book in computing history.

The book was finally authorized and republished in 1996, containing 1975 (V6) Unix source code and a newer 1977 commentary, updated by 9 additional people.

### Later Licensing, from ~1980 onwards

There were a number of global factors contributing to the eventual change in Unix licensing:

- As software became more complex, software production costs relative to hardware started increasing

- In the United States vs. IBM antitrust suit, filed 17 January 1969, the U.S. government charged that software bundled with hardware was anticompetitive

- Source code became copyrightable in the US in 1974. That, plus later court decisions such as {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apple_v._Franklin", "Apple v. Franklin") }} in 1983 for compiled (object) code, gave computer programs the copyright status of literary works. This started the closed-source software business model

- In the '80s, computer vendors and software-only companies began routinely charging for software licenses, marketing software as "Program Products" and imposing legal restrictions through copyrights, trademarks, and leasing contracts

- In 1983, IBM made a policy of no longer distributing source code with purchased software

And there were changes at AT&T as well, oriented towards increasing profits from Unix:

In 1979, AT&T announced {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Version_7_Unix", "Version 7 Unix") }} at USENIX, and the new academic/research license no longer permitted distributing source code to students in classes, which applied retroactively if/when universities upgraded to Version 7.

In 1981-1982, AT&T released {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Unix_System_III", "System III") }}, a commercialized Version 7 Unix plus additions, with a more straightforward licensing model. Four types of licenses existed:

1. "UNIX Academic / Educational Research Source License", the usual license for academic institutions, now with more restricted terms

2. "UNIX System III Source License (Agreement)", for customers wishing to run and modify their local Unix installation

3. "UNIX System III Object Code (Binary) License (Agreement)", for customers wishing to only run Unix in their environment

4. "UNIX System III Object Code (Binary) Distribution License (Agreement)", for customers wishing to embed and/or resell binaries to their customers

AT&T's binary distribution license included a fixed fee plus royalties per each copy sold. Unix vendors needed both the commercial source code license and a binary distribution license for their business model.
For the time being, customers who did not accept the new terms could still obtain V7 Unix and source code license from {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Western_Electric", "Western Electric") }}, and then with a valid license optionally also obtain a free BSD release with source code.

In 1982 came the {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Breakup_of_the_Bell_System", "break up of AT&T’s telecommunications monopoly") }}. Effective as of Jan 1, 1984, AT&T was free from the earlier 1956 and 1974 antitrust decrees, and was allowed to establish an unregulated division to sell computer hardware and software.

In parallel, Unix was becoming widespread. By 1984 there were 1400 universities using Unix, 70 lines of computers designed around Unix, and well over 100k systems running Unix on a variety of hardware.

AT&T discontinued the free distribution model and gradually increased costs. Educational source code licenses went from the initial $150 in the '70s to e.g. $800 for 1 CPU plus $400 per each additional CPU in 1983.
Commercial source code licenses went from $20k in the '70s to $43+k in 1983 per CPU (+$16k per additional CPU), $100+k in 1989, and $160+k in 1993, and still included no support.

### Unix Fragmentation

Separately from the mainline {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Research_Unix", "Research Unix") }} codebase, several groups within AT&T were maintaining their own forks of Unix. Those were primarily based on V5, V6, and V7 Unix, and included branches named {{ L("https://gunkies.org/wiki/USG_UNIX", "USG UNIX") }} (first released in 1973), {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/CB_UNIX", "CB UNIX") }} (1975), and {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/PWB/UNIX", "PWB/UNIX") }} (1977). There was also a real-time, {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Microkernel", "microkernel") }}-based branch of Unix available that ran under AT&T's {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Multi-Environment_Real-Time", "MERT") }} operating system (1977). This fragmentation was internal to AT&T.

The early public fragmentation happened when AT&T's original Unix source diverged into two or three main branches:

- {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Berkeley_Software_Distribution", "Berkeley Software Distribution (BSD)") }}, based on V6 Unix and first released in 1978, with source code (still requiring a source code license from AT&T, as usual)

- AT&T's {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Unix_System_III", "System III") }}, a commercialized unification of V7 Unix, {{ L("https://gunkies.org/wiki/USG_UNIX", "USG UNIX") }}, {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/CB_UNIX", "CB UNIX") }}, {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/PWB/UNIX", "PWB/UNIX") }}, and other additions, first released in 1981, with surcharge for source code

- AT&T's {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Unix_System_V", "System V") }}, a successor to System III, intended to further standardize and unify Unix, CB UNIX, and BSD, first released in 1983, with surcharge for source code

But, as mentioned, AT&T was also licensing Unix to outside parties. Licensees included universities such the mentioned University of California at Berkeley, Unix porting companies {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/UniSoft", "UniSoft") }} and {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Santa_Cruz_Operation", "Santa Cruz Operation (SCO)") }}, hardware vendors {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sun_Microsystems", "Sun Microsystems (Sun)") }}, {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/IBM", "IBM") }}, {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hewlett-Packard", "Hewlett Packard (HP)") }}, {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Digital_Equipment_Corporation", "Digital Equipment Corporation (DEC)") }}, {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Silicon_Graphics", "Silicon Graphics (SGI)") }}, {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dell", "Dell") }}, and countless others.

Licensees created their own variants of Unix by basing them on either BSD or one of AT&T's systems and adding proprietary extensions. Because the underlying licensor was always AT&T, these variants predominantly sold to end customers without source code because AT&T's binary-only licenses were significantly cheaper.

As a result, Unix, originally a single product with available source code, became divided into a number of separate, competing, closed-source, and incompatible offerings, all claiming superiority and planning to take over the market.

