### Unix Wars

As mentioned, AT&T was unsure how to best profit from Unix. In 1973 they established {{ L("https://gunkies.org/wiki/UNIX_Support_Group", "Unix Support Group (USG)") }} to provide service to AT&T departments that demanded support, which over time grew into a commercial offering. After 1982, when it was ruled that AT&T will be freed from the antitrust lawsuits and allowed to operate a computer business effective Jan 1, 1984, they tried to commercialize Unix through successive efforts {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/UNIX_System_V#SVR2", "Unix System Development Laboratory (USDL)") }} (1983), {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/AT%26T_Information_Systems", "AT&T Information Systems (ATTIS)") }} (1984), and finally {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Unix_System_Laboratories", "Unix System Laboratories (USL)") }} (1989).

AT&T hoped the increasing source license costs would eventually cause BSD users to switch to a binary, AT&T-based release.
By 1983-1984 AT&T incorporated more BSD features in its System V and in 1985 even based their V8 Research Unix on BSD, obtaining missing features such as the
{{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Berkeley_sockets", "Berkeley sockets") }}, {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Berkeley_Fast_File_System", "Berkeley Fast File System (FFS)") }},
and {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Virtual_memory", "virtual memory (swap)") }} support for {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/VAX", "DEC VAX") }}.

Starting with 1984, further distancing between Unix vendors and grouping into competing camps happened when:

- In 1984, {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/AT%26T_Computer_Systems", "AT&T Computer Systems (AT&T-CS)") }} division inherited {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/UNIX_System_V", "Unix System V") }} and positioned itself as a direct commercial competitor to existing Unix vendors

- In 1985, AT&T-CS issued its {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/System_V_Interface_Definition", "System V Interface Definition (SVID)") }}, requiring vendors calling their products "System V" to conform to it

- In 1986, as AT&T's computer divisions were generating losses, AT&T announced it would lay off 24k employees, mostly in ATTIS

- In 1987, European Unix vendors {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Groupe_Bull", "Bull") }}, ICL, {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Olivetti", "Olivetti") }}, Siemens, and Nixdorf created {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/X/Open", "X/Open") }} group to promote open standards and define a single specification for all operating systems derived from Unix

- In 1988, AT&T announced a 3-year plan to acquire {{ L("https://www.latimes.com/archives/la-xpm-1988-01-07-fi-33970-story.html", "up to 20% stake and a seat on the Board of Sun Microsystems") }}, "one of Silicon Valley’s most promising computer makers", to establish an "open technical standard"

- In 1988, {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Institute_of_Electrical_and_Electronics_Engineers", "IEEE Institute") }} published a separate, vendor-neutral {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/POSIX", "Portable Operating System Interface (POSIX)") }} standard for software compatibility between variants of operating systems. It was a compromise between System V and BSD, and to some extent a subset of SVID

- In 1988, AT&T and Sun Microsystems (who was until then a BSD-based vendor) released their Unix {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/System_V_Release_4", "System V Release 4") }}, unifying {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/System_V_Release_3#SVR3", "System V Release 3") }}, {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Xenix", "Xenix") }}, BSD, and {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sunos", "SunOS") }} features

- In 1988, AT&T and Sun Microsystems released their {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/OPEN_LOOK", "Open Look") }} GUI

- In 1988, a group of 7 vendors DEC, {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apollo_Computer", "Apollo") }}, {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Groupe_Bull", "Bull") }}, HP, IBM, Siemens, and Nixdorf, collectively called the Hamilton Group, created {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Open_Software_Foundation", "Open Software Foundation (OSF)") }} to protect themselves from the cooperation between AT&T and Sun Microsystems, combine code from their own {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/System_V_Release_3#SVR3", "System V Release 3") }} derivatives, and ultimately produce a Unix OS free of AT&T's intellectual property. "OSF" was humorously said to also stand for "Oppose Sun Forever"

- In 1988, AT&T and Sun Microsystems formalized their association as {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Unix_International", "Unix International (UI)") }}, to counter the establishment of OSF

- In 1989, OSF released their {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Motif_(software)", "Motif") }} GUI

- In 1989, AT&T, separately from its research Unix group at {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Murray_Hill,_New_Jersey", "Murray Hill") }}, and in cooperation with its Unix International and {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Novell", "Novell") }}, created {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Unix_System_Laboratories", "Unix System Laboratories (USL)") }} to once again try making its Unix business more profitable. AT&T transferred all its US-based Unix assets to USL, including the System V copyright and Unix trademark. Sun bought a stake in USL

- In 1989, due to an industry-wide recession and diminishing mainframe and minicomputer sales, most Unix vendors were in financial trouble. HP bought {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apollo_Computer", "Apollo Computer") }}

- In 1990, OSF released their first reference Unix implementation, {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/OSF/1", "OSF/1") }}

- In 1991, partly prompted by the recession, but also as a sort of giving up, and possibly even based on predicting the downfall of the Unix server and workstation market, {{ L("https://www.latimes.com/archives/la-xpm-1991-06-04-fi-223-story.html", "AT&T sold its stake in Sun Microsystems") }} as well as USL, "ending one of the most financially rewarding and technology-advancing relationships in the computer industry [with Sun]"

- In 1992, OSF gave up on the effort of producing their own Unix OS

Due to divergence, intellectual property, licensing issues, personal and corporate conflicts, commercial rivalry, and inability to agree
on a Unix standard, the period from ~1985 to ~1995 became collectively known as the period of {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Unix_wars", "Unix wars") }}.

### Outcomes of Unix Wars

Most of the efforts created during Unix wars ultimately had influential and valuable outcomes:

**POSIX** was in 1988 included in {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/National_Institute_of_Standards_and_Technology", "NIST") }} {{ L("https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/Legacy/FIPS/fipspub151.pdf", "FIPS 151") }}, requiring {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/POSIX", "POSIX") }} compliance for all Unixes utilized in the US government, making POSIX an industry standard relevant to this day.

**X/Open** was expanded by 1990 to include AT&T, DEC, HP, Sun, Unisys, NCR, Philips, Nokia, Prime, Apollo, Fujitsu, Hitachi, and NEC, and also both OSF and Unix International.
In 1994 X/Open managed to publish the {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Single_Unix_Specification", "Single Unix Specification (SUS)") }}, which in its core included the {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/POSIX", "POSIX") }} standard. X/Open was merged with OSF in 1996 to form {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Open_Group", "The Open Group") }}.

**Open Software Foundation (OSF)**'s OSF/1 operating system was released in 1990 for various platforms, demonstrating portability and vendor neutrality. Components included the {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mach_(kernel)", "CMU Mach 2.5 microkernel") }}, IBM journal filesystem, {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Net/1", "BSD networking code Net/1") }}, native virtual memory management, {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Motif_(software)", "Motif") }}, and {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Distributed_Computing_Environment", "Distributed Computing Environment (DCE)") }}. Other software vendors also licensed OSF/1, including Apple. Parts of OSF/1 are contained in so many versions of Unix that it may have been the most widely deployed Unix product.

**Unix International (UI)**, in cooperation with OSF, announced in 1993 the {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Common_Open_Software_Environment", "Common Open Software Environment (COSE)") }} initiative. Apart from the standardization effort, COSE's most notable product released in the same year was the industry-standard {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Common_Desktop_Environment", "Common Desktop Environment (CDE)") }}, based on the {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Motif_(software)", "Motif") }} toolkit. UI and COSE were merged in 1994 into OSF, and then under OSF in 1996 into the mentioned Open Group.

**OSF/Motif** was adopted in 1994 as a {{ L("https://diswww.mit.edu/menelaus.mit.edu/osf-news/9", "formal IEEE standard") }} for GUIs. OSF/Motif and CDE were merged into a single project, CDE/Motif, in 1995.

**Unix System Laboratories (USL)** and all its Unix assets were in 1992 bought by {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Novell", "Novell") }}, who was already USL's co-founder and wanted to incorporate Unix into its {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/NetWare", "NetWare network OS") }}. The purchase led to somewhat imprecise headlines that "Novell bought Unix", but the acquisition never really paid off and in 1995 Novell resold some Unix assets to {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Santa_Cruz_Operation", "Santa Cruz Operation (SCO)") }}, a vendor selling Unix variants for Intel x86 processors. Both USL and SCO indulged in lawsuits, to the detriment of Unix.

### Culmination of Unix Wars

But the last big event and a culmination of Unix wars was {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Unix_System_Laboratories,_Inc._v._Berkeley_Software_Design,_Inc.", "USL v. BSDi") }}, a 1992 lawsuit filed by AT&T's {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Unix_System_Laboratories", "Unix System Laboratories (USL)") }} against {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Berkeley_Software_Design", "Berkeley Software Design, Inc. (BSDi)") }}.

BSDi was a privately-owned company, established in 1991 by some members of the original {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Computer_Systems_Research_Group", "CSRG") }} group at Berkeley and others, including for example {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rick_Adams_(Internet_pioneer)", "Rick Adams") }}, the founder of {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/UUNET", "UUNET") }} (at one point the world's largest {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Internet_service_provider", "Internet Service Provider (ISP)") }}).

The events leading to the lawsuit were as follows:

As mentioned, CSRG at Berkeley was distributing BSD since 1978, with AT&T's approval, to interested parties who themselves had a valid Unix source code license from AT&T.

Ten years later, in 1988, since even AT&T's single-CPU source code licenses had become prohibitively expensive ($100+k) and Internet was growing, people expressed interest in obtaining networking code that would be developed outside of AT&T and not subject to AT&T's licensing.

CSRG audited the source code of their BSD TCP/IP stack and in 1989 released it to the general public as "{{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Net/1", "Net/1") }}", under a free and permissive {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/BSD_licenses", "BSD license") }}.

In 1991, after plans were announced to close CSRG, and following continuous {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Keith_Bostic_(software_engineer)", "Keith Bostic") }}'s initiative that started in 1986, CSRG made the final effort to remove all remaining AT&T code in BSD and replace it with their own, alternative implementation.
In June 1991, they made another public release under the BSD license, {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Net/2#Net/2_and_legal_troubles", "Net/2") }}. Net/2 was a nearly complete Unix-like system; it was only missing the implementation of 6 original kernel source files which instead contained empty function templates, and CSRG believed it not to contain any AT&T's intellectual property.

Berkeley Software Design, Inc. (BSDi) obtained the source of Net/2, performed a {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Clean_room_design", "clean room implementation") }} of the missing functions, and ported it to the Intel i386 computer architecture. By the end of 1991, BSDi started selling the resulting {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/BSD/OS", "BSD/386") }} operating system using a memorable telephone number "1-800-ITS-UNIX" and had ~300 customers. That usurped {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Unix_System_Laboratories", "USL") }}, who did not agree with the telephone number containing "UNIX" nor believed that BSDi's BSD/386 was free of USL's {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trade_secret", "trade secret") }}s.

(USL chose to dispute trade secrets rather than copyright because multiple authors were working on the code, code was sometimes developed jointly, and sometimes as derivative work which is not subject to original copyright. Trade secrets offered a broader legal ground -- they remain protected even if shared under a license (as long as they are not publicly disclosed), they were a good fit for what USL believed was misappropriated (the know-how, structure, design, etc.), and ultimately they should have helped to more easily get BSDi to stop distributing its software.)

USL filed a lawsuit against BSDi in April 1992, requesting sales of BSD/386 to stop until the case was resolved. BSDi countered, stating that only 6 out of ~18,000 files could be disputed, because the rest were already freely redistributable by UCB. Feeling that their case was weakened, USL refiled, this time including the University in it and requesting sales and distribution of both BSD/386 and Net/2 to stop.

In April 1993, as the judge did not grant the {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Injunction#Preliminary_injunctions", "preliminary injunction") }} against BSDi, BSDi continued selling BSD/386 at $595 for binary and $995 for source releases. They advertised a "99% discount" compared to AT&T's Unix System V.

Later in 1993, the University filed a countersuit against USL, claiming that USL had failed to credit the University for the use of BSD code in System V as required by the {{ L("https://www.crystallabs.io/software-licensing/#4-clause-bsd-bsd-original-bsd-old-bsd", "4-clause BSD license") }}. The University demanded that USL be forced to reprint all their documentation with the appropriate credit added, notify all their licensees of oversight, and run full-page advertisements in major publications, such as The Wall Street Journal and Fortune magazine, informing the public of their omission.

Soon after the countersuit, USL was purchased by {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Novell", "Novell") }}.

In 1994, after the judge expressed doubt in the validity of USL's trade secrets, and with Novell's Unix-friendly CEO {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ray_Noorda", "Ray Noorda") }} favoring a settlement, the case was settled out of court, with Novell and UCB agreeing not to litigate further over either BSD or users or distributors of future releases. Interestingly, {{ L("https://www.nokia.com/bell-labs/about/dennis-m-ritchie/bsdi/USLsettlement.pdf", "the full text of the USL-UCB settlement") }} remained secret until 2004, but can now be found along with other documents in {{ L("https://www.nokia.com/bell-labs/about/dennis-m-ritchie/bsdi/bsdisuit.html", "Dennis Ritchie's archive of USL vs. BSDi") }}.

As part of the settlement, of 18,000 files included in BSD, 3 had to be removed, and 70 modified to show the USL copyright notice. The first subsequent and free BSD release was {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/4.4BSD", "4.4BSD-Lite") }}, released in March 1994.

By 1995, {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/BSD/OS", "BSD/386") }} became the main Internet and datacenter OS. By the end of 2003, due to competition from open source BSDs and GNU/Linux, BSD/386 (in the meantime renamed BSD/OS) was discontinued.

### A Summary of Mistakes

Starting with 1990 onwards, almost all traditional Unix vendors have died out, either directly or have been bought and diluted in companies with no Unix heritage.

But their demise did not come overnight, as a mishap. It was a result of stubborn and insistently wrong decision making that was going on for a decade or more.

As mentioned earlier, most of the efforts created during Unix wars ultimately had influential and valuable outcomes. It is unfortunate that involved parties did not reconcile or start cooperating 5 to 10 years earlier.

But there were other problems:

Unix vendors were 5 to 10 years late in providing Unix on hardware that was, price-wise, accessible to the general public. Arguably they never provided this option, turning Unix into a niche.

{{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/X_Window_System", "X Window System") }}, the standard graphical windowing system for Unix, saw little joint development after the 1989 release of X11R4. Unix vendors were more interested in adding proprietary extensions than contributing back to the common code base released under the free and permissive {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/MIT_License", "MIT license") }}.

Unix vendors were 5 to 10 years late in reacting to an obvious industry trend -- the emergence of feeble {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Microcomputer", "microcomputers") }} and {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Personal_computer", "PCs") }}, whose popularity and agility far surpassed Unix vendors', made their business models obsolete, and eventually starved them of income and influence needed to respond.

There were people who understood the direction of the market. For example, {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Larry_McVoy", "Larry McVoy") }} and others warned Sun Microsystems in a 1993 letter titled {{ L("https://www.landley.net/history/mirror/unix/srcos.html", "The Sourceware Operating System Proposal") }}, but it did not have much effect. Besides, even that letter was authored 8 years after the 1985 release of {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/80386", "i386 CPU") }}, the first PC CPU reasonable enough to run Unix.

(Sun Microsystems did eventually try to open source Solaris in 2008 under the name {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/OpenSolaris", "OpenSolaris") }}, but without drivers for the diverse PC hardware and after Solaris had already switched to using the {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/GNOME", "GNOME") }} desktop. This effort was 10 to 15 years late to begin with, and was discontinued only two years later, immediately after the {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sun_acquisition_by_Oracle", "acquisition of Sun Microsystems by Oracle Corporation") }}.)

Another big, missed opportunity in the '90s and 2000s was related to graphical components {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Motif_(software)", "Motif") }} and {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Common_Desktop_Environment", "Common Desktop Environment (CDE)") }}. Motif and CDE had universal support and were arguably, alongside Unix itself, its flagship components. Since much of the early software running on GNU/Linux was ported from Unix, lack of Motif and CDE were significant showstoppers in porting GUI applications and having an industry-standard desktop quickly.
That did not only slow down the adoption of GNU/Linux, but it also deprived the shrinking Unix and Motif/CDE user groups of a completely new, massive market and community of users and contributors.

The lack of Motif was particularly frustrating. A free software project called {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/LessTif", "LessTif") }} tried to implement a replacement, but never quite succeeded. Motif and CDE were eventually released as free software, but only in 2012, after they have already lost all relevance and acquired so much of an outdated look and hatred for not being available that people have buried them together with the dinosaurs. Their release as free software was 10 to 20 years late.

Significant for the fate of traditional Unix was also the {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Unix_System_Laboratories,_Inc._v._Berkeley_Software_Design,_Inc.", "USL v. BSDi lawsuit") }}. It delayed BSD from 1992 to 1994, the years important for entering the general PC market, and it also once again undesirably brought Unix licensing into the spotlight.

Given the heavy burden of practically everything related to old-school Unix -- hardware, software, incompatibility, pricing, licensing, politics, elitism, and closed source -- it is maybe exactly just that all those variants are now relics of the past.

But, as a concept, Unix has defined all computing that we know to this day and continues to thrive free and receive enhancements like never before under the umbrellas of GNU/Linux and BSD.

## After Unix

### Plan9 and Inferno

After the 10th edition Unix in 1989, Bell Labs went back to doing more fundamental operating systems research.

In 1992 and 1996 they produced two more operating systems, {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Plan_9_from_Bell_Labs", "Plan 9") }} and {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Inferno_(operating_system)", "Inferno") }}.

{{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Plan_9_from_Bell_Labs", "Plan 9") }} developed Unix ideals further. Source code became compilable on all supported architectures, "{{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Everything_is_a_file", "everything is a file") }}" became true, userspace file servers and per-process namespaces were added, and the lack of location-agnostic kernel APIs (which resulted in the creation of separate {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Berkeley_sockets", "Berkeley sockets") }} API, {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ptrace", "ptrace") }}, and other syscalls in Unix) was fixed.

In summary, {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Plan_9_from_Bell_Labs", "Plan 9") }} implemented {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Distributed_computing", "distributed computing") }} -- the sharing of CPUs, filesystems, and devices across computers and architectures as if they were local. This was enabled by processing happening transparently on nodes that owned the data and devices they were exporting.

{{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Inferno_(operating_system)", "Inferno") }}'s focus was on bringing ideas from Plan 9 to a wider range of users and devices, including web browsers, with cross-platform execution based on the {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dis_virtual_machine", "Dis virtual machine (VM)") }}. Inferno could have been a platform competing with {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Java_(programming_language)", "Java") }}, but its model depended on the Dis VM, the Inferno OS, and the {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Limbo_(programming_language)", "Limbo programming language") }}.

Plan 9 and Inferno never gained traction and were even formally discontinued in 2015. Apart from early licensing issues, there was no Unix compatibility, the development environment was strange, and the industry did not want to go back 15 to 20 years in time; Unix was already there, it was good enough, and could gradually receive improvements.

But many of their concepts propagated back to Unix and general computing, including the {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Procfs", "proc filesystem") }}, {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fork_(system_call)#Rfork", "clone/rfork syscall") }}, {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Utf-8", "UTF-8") }}, {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Linux_namespaces", "namespaces") }}, {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Compositing_window_manager", "compositing") }}, {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/RandR#Other_DDX_components", "xrandr") }}, and the {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/9P_(protocol)", "9P protocol") }}. A project named {{ L("https://9fans.github.io/plan9port/", "Plan 9 from User Space") }} maintains ports of other, less-known Plan 9 ideas and programs for Unix.

The {{ L("https://plan9foundation.org/", "Plan 9 Foundation") }} was established in late 2020, covering Plan 9 and Inferno, and organizing the yearly {{ L("https://iwp9.org/", "International Workshop on Plan 9") }}. In 2021, Plan 9 and Inferno source code were released under the {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/MIT_License", "MIT license") }}.

Plan 9 has an actively developed fork for skilled hobbyists called {{ L("https://9front.org/", "9front") }}.
There are a couple YouTube channels available, such as Andrew Gibson's {{ L("https://www.youtube.com/@adventuresin9", "@adventuresin9") }}.
The {{ L("https://fqa.9front.org/fqa0.html", "first section of 9front's FQA") }} contains a very useful and informative overview, including
an answer to why random users might want to use 9front: "It is very likely that they don't". 

### GNU - GNU's Not Unix

In 1971, {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Richard_Stallman", "Richard Stallman") }}, a freshman at Harvard, began working for Russell Noftsker at the {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/MIT_AI_Lab", "MIT Artificial Intelligence Lab") }}. His job was to develop and improve the {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Incompatible_Timesharing_System", "ITS") }} operating system used by the Lab.

Work and research at the Lab were originally being done in the same technical, social, and ethical hacker culture that was present in computing since its beginnings in the 1950s, but as the '80s were approaching Stallman noticed concerning trends:

In 1980, Noftsker and his group that separated from MIT formed {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Symbolics_Inc.", "Symbolics Inc.") }} to design and manufacture {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lisp_machine", "Lisp machines") }}, single-user computers optimized for running the programming language {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lisp_(programming_language)", "Lisp") }}. Symbolics hired 14 MIT AI Lab staff, no longer visited there, and did not share the code of their first edition in 1982.
Stallman made a different, parallel implementation to prevent the company from gaining a monopoly, and shared the work with others in the continued "spirit of scientific collaboration and openness".

Also in 1982, the AI Lab bought a new {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/PDP-10", "PDP-10") }} and its administrators decided to use Digital's non-free timesharing system {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tops-20", "TOPS-20") }} instead of {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Incompatible_Timesharing_System", "ITS") }}. The new OS required signing a nondisclosure agreement even for binaries, which made any kind of sharing or collaborative improvements impossible.

Finally, Stallman had previously modified the Lab's printer driver to notify owners of active print jobs when paper was jammed. When Xerox donated a high-speed laser printer to the Lab and the same modification was necessary, Stallman surprisingly found no driver source code available, and a person that had it refused to share it due to a {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Non-disclosure_agreement", "non-disclosure agreement (NDA)") }}.

Stallman was in total opposition of non-disclosure agreements, new licensing terms, increasing incompatibilities between vendors, lack of source code, and loss of user freedoms.
To him, "the idea of proprietary software was antisocial, unethical, and simply wrong, dividing the public and keeping users helpless".

To overturn the situation, Stallman made a definite decision to create a Unix-like system, but replace all components of Unix with {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Free_software", "Free Software") }} ("Free" as in freedom).

Free Software refers to software that users have the freedom to run in any environment and for any purpose, as well as to study, share, and modify it, and distribute their modified versions.
Computer programs are deemed "free" if they give end users complete control over their software and, subsequently, over their devices.

In 1983, {{ L("https://www.gnu.org/gnu/initial-announcement.html", "Stallman announced the GNU project") }} on "net.unix-wizards" and "net.usoft" {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Usenet", "Usenet") }} newsgroups. The name "GNU" was chosen as an acronym for "GNU's Not Unix".

In 1984, he resigned from MIT, so that MIT could not claim copyright over his work on {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/GNU", "GNU software") }}. But thanks to {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Patrick_Winston", "Patrick Henry Winston") }}, the director of the MIT AI Lab from 1972 to 1997, Stallman continued to have access to office, lab space, and computing facilities.

In January 1984, software development for the GNU operating system began. In 1984 and 1985, GNU Emacs text editor started being usable and was available for download on Stallman's computer *prep.ai.mit.edu* via FTP.
People who were not on the Internet could order a physical tape from Stallman for a fee of $150. That became a free software distribution business and a precursor to companies that today distribute entire GNU/Linux-based systems.

By mid-1984, GNU already had an impressive collection of software.

In October 1985, the {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Free_Software_Foundation", "Free Software Foundation (FSF)") }} was founded to support the {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Free_software_movement", "free software movement") }}, employ software developers to write free software for the GNU Project, and work on the legal and structural elements of free software.

In March 1985, the {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/GNU_manifesto", "GNU Manifesto") }} call-to-action was popularized after publishing in {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dr._Dobb's_Journal", "Dr. Dobb's Journal of Software Tools") }}. Its text is available in {{ L("https://www.gnu.org/gnu/manifesto.html", "the GNU Manifesto") }}.

In February 1986, the {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Free_Software_Definition", "Free Software Definition") }} was published. Its text is available in {{ L("https://www.gnu.org/philosophy/free-sw.html", "What is Free Software?") }}.

In February 1989, the {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/GNU_General_Public_License", "GNU General Public License (GPL)") }} was published. Its text is available in {{ L("https://www.gnu.org/licenses/gpl-3.0.html", "GNU General Public License") }}.

Starting with {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/GNU_Emacs", "GNU Emacs") }} and {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/GNU_Compiler_Collection", "GNU Compiler Collection (GCC)") }}, by the early 1990s numerous GNU programs became de-facto standard and, thanks to their quality, free license, unhindered development, and better portability and support, quickly replaced their obsolete Unix counterparts.

### Linux

{{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Linux_kernel", "Linux") }} is a free and open source, Unix-like {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kernel_(operating_system)", "kernel") }} that was created in 1991 by {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Linus_Torvalds", "Linus Torvalds") }}, a then 21-year-old computer science student at the University of Helsinki.

At the time of the first Linux release in 1991, the circumstances were as follows:

- In 1983, Stallman created {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/GNU_Project", "the GNU Project") }}. GNU had produced almost everything needed for a complete operating system, but the intended kernel {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/GNU_Hurd", "GNU Hurd") }} wasn't ready

- In 1985, Intel released 80386, the first x86 CPU that had a 32-bit instruction set and a {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Memory_management_unit", "memory management unit (MMU)") }} with {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paging", "paging") }}

- In 1986, Maurice J. Bach of AT&T released {{ L("https://github.com/jyfc/ebook/blob/master/03_operating_system/the_design_of_the_unix_operating_system.pdf", "The Design of the Unix Operating System") }}. The book covered {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/System_V#SVR2", "System V Release 2") }} kernel and some new features from {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/System_V#SVR3", "System V Release 3") }} and BSD

- In 1987, {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Minix", "MINIX") }} was released as a Unix-like system for academic use. But Minix was 16-bit and, although source code was available, the license prevented modification and redistribution

- Any Unixes running on PCs were too expensive

- And the open source BSD for PCs, {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/386BSD", "386BSD") }}, was not yet available; it only became available in March 1992

So in July 1991 {{ L("https://www.linux.com/news/linuss-famous-email/", "Linus Torvalds announced") }} the first version of a {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Unix-like", "Unix-like") }} kernel named {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Linux", "Linux") }}.

Linux did not use any Unix code and was, like GNU, free from Unix' architectural and legal problems.

As Torvalds was unable to find {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/POSIX", "POSIX") }} documentation for {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/System_call", "system calls") }}, he modeled them after references in his university's {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sunos", "SunOS") }} documentation.

In 1992, Orest Zborowski ported the standard Unix GUI framework, {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/X11", "X Window System (X11)") }}, to Linux. He made a good decision not to adapt X to Linux, but to make Linux more {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Unix_System_V", "System V") }}-like, to be able to compile X with as little modification as possible. The improvements and the availability of X11 made Linux serious quickly. More about that porting project can be read in {{ L("https://www.linuxjournal.com/article/70", "Interview: Orest Zborowski") }}.

A more complete history of Linux can be read in the {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/History_of_Linux", "History of Linux") }}.

Linux is free software and a current/ongoing project; for any additional information please see {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Linux", "Linux page at Wikipedia") }}.

### GNU/Linux

Stallman needed a kernel, and Torvalds needed all other software for a usable operating system.

In 1991 Torvalds attended Stallman's talk on {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Free_software", "Free Software") }} where they've met.

The combined, complete Unix-like system was named GNU/Linux. Because everything was {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Free_software", "Free Software") }} and everyone was free to use, modify, and redistribute their modified system, as early as 1992 at least 5 {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gnu/linux", "GNU/Linux") }} distributions became available.

In 1997, Torvalds said in an interview that releasing Linux under the free and open source {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/GNU_General_Public_License", "GNU General Public License (GPL)") }} was "definitely the best thing [he] ever did".

Today, GNU/Linux is the operating system running on 100% of {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/TOP500", "Top 500") }} computer systems in the world. In previous decades those systems were running some version of Unix.

In June 2023, {{ L("https://www.tomshardware.com/news/linux-hits-3-percent-client-pc-market-share", "GNU/Linux reached 3% market share on the desktop") }}, and in July 2024 {{ L("https://www.tomshardware.com/software/linux/linux-market-share-approaching-45-for-first-time-could-hit-5-by-1q25", "GNU/Linux reached 4.5% market share on the desktop") }}.

GNU/Linux is free software and a current/ongoing project; for any additional information please see {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gnu/linux", "GNU/Linux page at Wikipedia") }}.

## Additional Information

This chapter provides isolated pieces of information that are separate from the main storyline, but are still important for understanding Unix, its history, or influence.

### 386BSD

{{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Berkeley_Software_Design", "BSDi") }}'s commercial operating system {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/BSD/OS", "BSD/386") }} (later renamed BSD/OS) was not the only spinoff from {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Computer_Systems_Research_Group", "CSRG") }}.

One of BSDi's founders was {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/William_Jolitz", "William Jolitz") }}. As the release of BSD/386 in late 1991 was nearing, Jolitz supposedly discovered that BSDi planned not to release BSD/386 under a free BSD license.

Unhappy with the fact, Jolitz removed the code he had done for BSD/386 and left BSDi to organize a competing and free operating system, somewhat confusingly named {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/386BSD", "386BSD") }}.

In 1992, {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/William_Jolitz", "William Jolitz") }} and his wife {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lynne_Jolitz", "Lynne Jolitz") }} released 386BSD versions 0.0 and 0.1. Users began producing bug fixes and enhancements, releasing them as unofficial patches.

In 1993, disagreeing with Jolitz on the future direction and releases of 386BSD, one group of users forked to create the {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/FreeBSD", "FreeBSD") }} project.

Also in 1993, another group of users forked to create the {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/NetBSD", "NetBSD") }} project, with the goal of reuniting 386BSD and other BSDs into a common OS with good multi-platform support.

386BSD released version 1.0 in 1994, and after a period of significant popularity in 1995-1996 it was forgotten by the 2000s.

{{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/NetBSD", "NetBSD") }} and its fork {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/OpenBSD", "OpenBSD") }}, and {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/FreeBSD", "FreeBSD") }} and its forks {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/GhostBSD", "GhostBSD") }} and {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/DragonFly_BSD", "DragonFly BSD") }}, are active and most popular BSD Unix distributions available today.

### BSD and GNU/Linux Differences

Two biggest branches of traditional Unix, BSD and System V, have diverged over time. Even their userland utilities are in some cases significantly different.
For example, `ps aux` on BSD and `ps -ef` on SysV are equivalent commands to display all system processes.

GNU-based utilities have bridged that gap by mostly supporting both styles and also adding their own, more uniform and modern style where it made sense.

But in terms of BSD and GNU/Linux, the primary differences can be summarized as follows:

1. Look & feel-wise, BSDs take pride in having a more direct Unix lineage. They also require more knowledge to install and use, have less hardware support, and a smaller user base

1. Licensing-wise, even though {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/BSD_licenses", "BSD licenses") }}, a similar {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/MIT_License", "MIT License") }}, and {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/GNU_General_Public_License", "GNU GPL licenses") }} are all {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Permissive_free_software_license", "free software licenses") }}, they have a key difference:

BSD and MIT licenses are very permissive -- they do not require source code to be available, and they allow reuse and relicensing of code as proprietary software.
The only requirement sometimes is that the relicensed software includes a copy of the original license and copyright notice.

On the other hand, {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/GNU_General_Public_License", "GNU GPL") }} and variants are {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Copyleft", "copyleft") }} licenses.
They guarantee users four basic freedoms -- to use, study, modify, and share software.
As such, they require making source code available and require that, if GPL code is modified and redistributed, it is done under the same or equivalent license terms as the original.

This copyleft protection provided by GPL was crucial to the success of {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gnu/linux", "GNU/Linux") }} and {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Free_software", "Free Software") }} in general. Copyleft licenses guarantee that software will remain free and benefit users indefinitely, rather than be taken by commercial companies for free, and then modified and resold for profit and under non-free licensing terms.

Worth clarifying is that GNU GPL does not prevent commercialization. It is perfectly fine to sell free software and any support services around it -- the only requirement is that users' rights, guaranteed by the GPL license, remain unchanged. Or, if companies do not wish to pass GPL rights to users, they can request from authors a paid, commercial license that will allow them to make proprietary modifications and resell under non-free terms.

### The Term "Open Source"

In 1998, {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Netscape", "Netscape") }} released their web browser {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Netscape_Navigator", "Netscape Navigator") }} as {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Open-source_software", "Open Source Software") }}. Netscape and its {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Netscape_Communicator", "Netscape Communicator") }} suite are the ancestors of today's {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mozilla_Foundation", "Mozilla Foundation") }} and its products like the {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Firefox", "Firefox") }} web browser and {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mozilla_Thunderbird", "Thunderbird") }} e-mail client.

However, at the time of Netscape's release, the term {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Open-source_software", "Open Source Software (OSS)") }} did not yet exist. Netscape's publishing the source code had prompted a meeting at which the term was coined.

Later in 1998, {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bruce_Perens", "Bruce Perens") }} and {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eric_S._Raymond", "Eric S. Raymond") }} founded the non-profit {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Open_Source_Initiative", "Open Source Initiative (OSI)") }} as a steward organization for the {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Open-source-software_movement", "Open Source Software movement") }}. OSI's website is {{ L("https://opensource.org/") }}.

OSI maintains the {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Open_Source_Definition", "Open Source Definition") }} and the corresponding open source certification mark.

The terms Free Software and Open Source are often used interchangeably in informal speech, but they are different. The differences can be summarized as follows:

- The term *Open Source* was coined to help promote the open source business model to companies who did not understand or care about {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Free_software", "Free Software") }}

- Open Source is focused on benefits for businesses, while Free Software is focused on benefits for users

- Open Source is utilitarian and concerns itself with the availability of source code, while Free Software concerns itself with user freedoms (of which source code availability is one)

- Open Source movement accepts the existence of proprietary software, while Free Software movement considers all non-free software bad

In short, all Free Software qualifies as Open Source, but only a subset of Open Source qualifies as Free Software.

### SCO versus GNU/Linux

Around 2003, {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_SCO_Group", "The SCO Group") }}, who bought some Unix assets from Novell in 1995, was used as a tool to start {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/SCO%E2%80%93Linux_disputes", "a series of legal disputes between SCO and Linux vendors") }}.

In 2003, in {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/SCO_v._IBM", "SCO v. IBM") }}, SCO sued IBM over code contributed to the Linux kernel. SCO claimed that part of it was SCO's intellectual property, asking between $1B and $5B for damages, and aiming to collect royalties from Linux. They went as far as to start offering big customers to pay for Linux at a discount quickly, rather than be confronted with high costs later.

The lawsuits had no real chance of succeeding; their purpose was to slow down Linux' rise in popularity by any means, including creating operational difficulties and spreading {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fear%2C_uncertainty_and_doubt", "fear, uncertainty, and doubt (FUD)") }}. The applicability and effectiveness of this method on software was already proven, unintentionally, a decade earlier during Unix Wars.

In 2007, a long four years later, in {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/SCO_v._Novell", "SCO v. Novell") }}, it was ruled that Novell did not transfer the Unix copyright to SCO, invalidating the root of SCO's claims and leading it to bankruptcy by 2012.

### MINIX

{{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Minix", "MINIX") }} was a Unix-like operating system first created in 1987. Professor {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Andrew_S._Tanenbaum", "Andrew Tanenbaum") }} created MINIX in response to the change in licensing of AT&T's V7 Unix, to be able to continue teaching Unix in classes.

Due to technical limitations and a non-free license, MINIX did not progress beyond a tool for students and hobbyists. In 2000 it was retroactively relicensed under a free and open source BSD license and abandoned over the next couple years.

However, what prompts a section on MINIX is that it surprisingly remains an active and widely deployed operating system. Without informing Tanenbaum of exact purpose, Intel commissioned him for some work on MINIX 3, then further customized the codebase and deployed it in all Intel CPUs after August 2015. MINIX is running on the Intel {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Platform_Controller_Hub", "PCH") }} {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Intel_Management_Engine", "ME") }} CPU, one of at least 4 internal CPUs on Intel motherboards and {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/System_on_a_chip", "SoC") }}s that are always running and are not accessible to users, but have access to computer's memory, storage, and networking.

## Current Unix, BSD, GNU, and Linux Offerings

Surviving proprietary Unix implementations are:

- {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oracle_Solaris", "Oracle Solaris") }}, based on {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Unix_System_V", "Unix System V") }}

- {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/HP-UX", "HP-UX") }}, {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Single_Unix_Specification#2001:_Single_Unix_Specification,_version_3,_POSIX.1-2001", "SUSv3") }}-certified and based on {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Unix_System_V", "Unix System V") }}

- {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/IBM_AIX", "IBM AIX") }}, {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Single_Unix_Specification#2001:_Single_Unix_Specification,_version_3,_POSIX.1-2001", "SUSv3") }}-certified and based on {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Unix_System_V", "Unix System V") }}

- {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/MacOS", "Apple macOS") }}, {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Single_Unix_Specification#2001:_Single_Unix_Specification,_version_3,_POSIX.1-2001", "SUSv3") }}-certified and based on {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Darwin_(operating_system)", "Darwin") }}, a derivative of {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Berkeley_Software_Distribution#Standards", "4.4BSD-Lite2") }} and {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/FreeBSD", "FreeBSD") }}

Open source SysV distributions, descended from the discontinued {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/OpenSolaris", "OpenSolaris") }} and its successor {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Illumos", "Illumos") }}, are:

- {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/OpenIndiana", "OpenIndiana") }}, a desktop-friendly Illumos distribution for servers

- {{ L("https://omnios.org/", "OmniOS") }}, a minimalist Illumos distribution for servers, just enough for tools and the OS to be self-supported

- {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/SmartOS", "SmartOS") }}, an {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/System_V_Release_4#SVR4", "SVR4") }}-based {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hypervisor", "hypervisor") }}

Most popular open source BSD implementations are:

- {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/FreeBSD", "FreeBSD") }}

- {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/NetBSD", "NetBSD") }}

- {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/OpenBSD", "OpenBSD") }}

- {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/GhostBSD", "GhostBSD") }}

- {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/DragonFly_BSD", "DragonFly BSD") }}

Many other BSD distributions can be seen listed in {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_BSD_operating_systems", "List of BSD operating systems") }} and {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Comparison_of_BSD_operating_systems", "Comparison of BSD operating systems") }}.

And finally, open source GNU/Linux implementations are too numerous and diverse to list. We could mention just some:

- {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Debian", "Debian GNU") }}, arguably the "best" and 2nd oldest active GNU/Linux distribution, adhering to {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Debian_Free_Software_Guidelines", "Debian Free Software Guidelines (DFSG)") }}

- {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ubuntu", "Ubuntu") }}, a more commercially-oriented distribution, based on Debian

- {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Devuan", "Devuan GNU+Linux") }}, a fork of Debian that uses sysvinit, runit, or OpenRC instead of systemd

- {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pop!_OS", "Pop! OS") }}, a desktop-first distribution, based on Ubuntu, made by {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/System76", "System76") }}

- {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Linux_Mint", "Linux Mint") }}, a popular community-developed distribution with flavors based on Ubuntu and Debian, with a different default desktop environment

The above list is limited and only mentions Linux distributions derived from Debian. Many other distributions can be seen listed in {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_Linux_distributions", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_Linux_distributions") }} and {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Comparison_of_Linux_distributions", "Comparison of Linux distributions") }}.

A broader comparison of all open source operating systems can be seen in the {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Comparison_of_open_source_operating_systems", "Comparison of open-source operating systems") }}.

## Interesting Sources, Projects, and Teaching Materials

Because Unix and BSD source code can be traced back to its roots in the 1970s, {{ L("https://www.spinellis.gr/", "Diomidis Spinellis") }} created a Git-based {{ L("https://github.com/dspinellis/unix-history-repo", "Unix History Repository") }} that contains continuous Unix commit history from its beginnings to today.

As mentioned, John Lions' wrote the first {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lions%27_Commentary_on_UNIX_6th_Edition,_with_Source_Code", "Commentary on the UNIX Operating System") }} ({{ L("https://archive.org/details/bitsavers_attunix6thtaryontheUnixOperatingSystem197705_12314928", "Commentary's alternative PDF") }}) in 1977.

A modern, free, and educational reimplementation of V6 Unix from Lions' Commentary is available and named {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Xv6", "xv6") }}.

A secure, fast, static-architecture OS based on the {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/L4_microkernel_family#High_assurance:_seL4", "seL4") }} {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Microkernel", "microkernel") }}, currently in its early stage, is available and named the {{ L("https://lionsos.org/", "Lions Operating System (LionsOS)") }}.

Andrew Warkentin is working on a QNX-like OS based on the {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/L4_microkernel_family#High_assurance:_seL4", "seL4") }} {{ L("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Microkernel", "microkernel") }}, currently in its early stage, called {{ L("https://gitlab.com/uxrt/uxrt-toplevel", "UX/RT") }}.

Oscar Vermeulen is producing {{ L("https://obsolescence.dev/", "orderable replicas of DEC PDP computers") }}.
